<article class="article-body">

<p>"It's risky to give someone this much access" is the single most common hesitation I hear from founders considering a remote chief of staff, and it's a reasonable one. The role sits close to financials, personnel decisions, and investor conversations by design. The question isn't whether that risk is real, it's whether the engagement is structured to manage it properly. Here's what that structure actually looks like.</p>

<h2>A mutual NDA before any real detail is shared</h2>
<p>Confidentiality protection starts before the discovery call goes deep, not after an engagement begins. A mutual NDA, signed before sensitive details are discussed, means both sides are protected from the first substantive conversation, not just once a contract is signed weeks later.</p>

<h2>Client-owned systems, not the chief of staff's own tools</h2>
<p>A properly structured engagement runs on systems you own and control: your CRM, your document storage, your communication channels. The alternative, a chief of staff running everything through their own personal tools, means your data lives somewhere you don't control and can't fully audit. Client-owned systems also make offboarding clean: access gets revoked the same way any employee's would.</p>

<h2>Encrypted storage and basic security hygiene, as a baseline not a favor</h2>
<p>This shouldn't be a negotiating point. Encrypted storage, two-factor authentication enforced on anything touching your business, and no mixing of personal and client channels are table stakes, not premium add-ons. If a candidate hasn't already thought about this before you ask, that's worth noting.</p>

<h2>Written decision rights, so access maps to authority</h2>
<p>Confidentiality isn't only about who can see information, it's about who can act on it. A written decision-rights document, agreed early, spells out what a chief of staff can decide independently versus what requires sign-off. This protects the founder from surprises and protects the chief of staff from being blamed for decisions that were never actually theirs to make.</p>

<h2>A clear data-retention plan for when the engagement ends</h2>
<p>Engagements end, and what happens to access and data at that point should be agreed before it starts, not figured out on the way out. A clear end-of-engagement plan, revoking access, returning or deleting files, confirming no copies remain on personal devices, is part of a properly structured arrangement, not an afterthought.</p>

<h2>What to confirm before you sign anything</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A mutual NDA</strong>, executed before deep discovery, not after</li>
  <li><strong>Client-owned systems</strong> for anything touching sensitive data</li>
  <li><strong>Encrypted storage and enforced 2FA</strong> as a default, not a special request</li>
  <li><strong>Written decision rights</strong> that map access to actual authority</li>
  <li><strong>An agreed data-retention and offboarding plan</strong> before the engagement starts</li>
</ul>

<p>This is how every engagement I run is structured, not because it's best practice in the abstract, but because the work genuinely requires that level of trust to be useful. You hold the keys the entire time. Read more in the <a href="/faq/">full FAQ</a>, or <a href="/booking/">book a free 30-minute call</a> and ask about this directly before anything else.</p>

